# LiveKit API keys for the SHARED, multi-tenant LiveKit — one key per customer.
#
# Copy to livekit-keys.yaml, which is gitignored, and chmod it 600:
#
#   cp livekit-keys.yaml.sample livekit-keys.yaml
#   chmod 600 livekit-keys.yaml
#
# livekit-server REFUSES to start if the file is readable by others — it exits with
# "key file others permissions must be set to 0". That is deliberate on LiveKit's
# part and worth leaving alone: the file holds every tenant's signing secret.
#
# WHY ONE KEY PER TENANT. A LiveKit token is signed with the API secret and names
# the room it grants access to. With a single shared secret, any tenant that has it
# can mint a token for ANY room on this server, including another tenant's, and can
# ask for roomAdmin while doing so. The shared-host provisioner wrote the same
# key and secret into every customer's .env, so that was the state. That model,
# and the script, are gone: every customer now has their own server and their own
# LiveKit, and `make secrets` generates their signing key on their machine.
#
# Each customer's own .env carries only its own pair:
#   LIVEKIT_API_KEY_NAME=oc-<cid>
#   LIVEKIT_API_PASS=<that customer's secret>
#   LIVEKIT_API_KEY="oc-<cid>: <that customer's secret>"
#
# Append the new line here by hand and restart LiveKit.
# Removing a customer's line revokes their ability to create tokens, which is the
# other half of why this is a file and not a constant.
#
# Format is "<name>: <secret>", one per line. Secrets should be at least 32
# characters: openssl rand -base64 32

# oc-acme: replace-with-openssl-rand-base64-32
# oc-globex: replace-with-openssl-rand-base64-32
